      "targeted_response_start_logprob": 3.0178
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While extreme \"conspiracy\" readings are exaggerated, the concern stems from a valid fear of spatial control. Critics argue that hyper-localization can lead to social segregation, effectively trapping people within specific socioeconomic zones. If access to essential services becomes rigidly dependent on very short distances, it can restrict mobility for those without personal transport or those whose needs fall outside the established local loop. This skepticism highlights the tension between convenience and genuine freedom of movement in urban planning.",
      "source": "target",
